<Base title="About" active="about" description="What DataCenter Atlas tracks and how it works">
  <header class="page-head">
    <div>
      <h1 class="page-title">About</h1>
      <p class="page-sub">What DataCenter Atlas tracks and how it works</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <article class="about panel">
    <p>
      DataCenter Atlas tracks the data center industry across Asia-Pacific: a daily
      AI-curated news digest, a live database of projects and facilities, and a directory
      of the companies behind them.
    </p>

    <h2>Latest — the daily digest</h2>
    <p>
      An automated pipeline polls industry publications, Google News queries and company
      newsrooms every day. An AI pass clusters duplicate coverage of the same story,
      scores each story for relevance and importance (0–100), and writes the summaries
      and "why it matters" notes. Full article text is extracted where the publisher
      allows it, so you can read without leaving the site; every story still credits and
      links the original reporting. The "sources" count on trending stories is the number
      of distinct publications covering the same story.
    </p>
    <p>Coverage is organized into five APAC regions, plus a Global bucket:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>China</strong> (including Hong Kong)</li>
      <li><strong>Japan</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Korea</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Australia</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Southeast Asia</strong> — Singapore, Malaysia, Indonesia, Thailand, Vietnam, the Philippines, and neighbors</li>
      <li><strong>Global</strong> — stories outside the region that materially affect APAC, such as GPU supply or global cloud capex</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Pipeline — the project database</h2>
    <p>
      The project database combines two kinds of sources: concrete project events
      (groundbreakings, expansions, financing, commissioning) extracted from the tracked
      news, and bulk facility inventories imported from public directories. Each project
      page shows its status, capacity, investment, key dates and specs where disclosed,
      plus the timeline of news that mentioned it.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Status</strong> is the normalized lifecycle stage: announced → under construction → operational (plus on hold / cancelled). Source-specific labels like "prospective expansion" or "land bank" show up as qualifier chips.</li>
      <li><strong>Capacity</strong> is IT load in MW. "Total planned" figures cover full campus build-out, so a live phase can be smaller. Leased tenant deployments are counted on the host facility, not double-counted.</li>
      <li><strong>Last updated</strong> is the date of the last real change (new linked story, status change, new field) — not the date data was bulk-imported.</li>
      <li>Directory entries not yet confirmed by tracked news are marked as such; treat single-source directory data as indicative.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Companies — the operator directory</h2>
    <p>
      The directory aggregates the project database by operator: how many facilities,
      how much capacity, which markets. Companies with a substantial tracked footprint
      (or editorial significance) get curated profiles with logos and descriptions;
      smaller operators appear under "Other operators" once they pass ten tracked
      facilities. News stories are tagged with the companies they concern, and those
      tags link here.
    </p>

    <h2>Saved — your bookmarks</h2>
    <p>
      The bookmark icon on any story or project saves it for later; the Saved toggle on
      the Latest and Pipeline pages filters to your bookmarks. Bookmarks live in your
      browser's local storage — no account, no tracking, and they don't leave your
      device.
    </p>

    <h2>A note on scores and data</h2>
    <p>
      Scores, summaries, and "why it matters" notes are AI-generated heuristics designed
      to help you skim — they are not editorial judgment, investment advice, or a
      substitute for reading the original reporting. Facility data from public
      directories can lag reality. Always follow the source links for the full picture.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
